```java
/**
 * Queueable class that pushes newly created Accounts to the ERP,
 * one POST per Account so the ERP team can trace every record.
 *
 * The endpoint accepts 1-200 customers per call; we intentionally
 * send 1 customer per request.
 *
 * Salesforce enforces a 10-callout limit per transaction, so for
 * batches of up to 200 accounts we chain executions in groups of 10.
 */
public class ErpCustomerSync implements Queueable {

    private static final Integer MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_EXECUTION = 10;

    private final List<Id> accountIds;

    public ErpCustomerSync(List<Id> accountIds) {
        this.accountIds = accountIds;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) return;

        // Deterministic ordering so chained re-enqueues resume correctly.
        List<Account> accounts = [
            SELECT Id, Name, BillingCountry
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
            ORDER BY Id
        ];

        Integer processed = 0;

        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            if (processed >= MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_EXECUTION) break;

            // country must be null when blank
            String country = String.isNotBlank(acc.BillingCountry)
                ? acc.BillingCountry
                : null;

            Map<String, Object> customer = new Map<String, Object>{
                'salesforceId' => acc.Id,
                'name'         => acc.Name,
                'country'      => country
            };

            // Body is always a JSON array containing exactly one customer
            List<Map<String, Object>> body = new List<Map<String, Object>>{ customer };

            HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
            req.setEndpoint('callout:ERP/api/customers');
            req.setMethod('POST');
            req.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
            req.setBody(JSON.serialize(body));

            Http http = new Http();
            HttpResponse res = http.send(req);
            // Expected: 202 Accepted. Error handling / retries are out of scope.
            // Log if useful: system.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN, 'ERP response ' + res.getStatusCode());

            processed++;
        }

        // Re-enqueue remaining accounts (e.g. 200-account marketplace batch
        // requires 20 chained executions of 10 callouts each).
        if (processed < accounts.size()) {
            List<Id> remaining = new List<Id>();
            for (Integer i = processed; i < accounts.size(); i++) {
                remaining.add(accounts[i].Id);
            }
            System.enqueueJob(new ErpCustomerSync(remaining));
        }
    }
}
```

### Why Queueable instead of a truly synchronous callout

The task says *"make the callout right from the trigger."* In Apex there is no supported way to issue HTTP callouts inside a synchronous trigger context when the transaction has just performed DML (the insert itself). The platform either throws `Callouts are not allowed in trigger context` or, in some releases, silently blocks the request. More importantly, a 200-account batch would require 200 callouts while the governor limit is **10 callouts per synchronous transaction**, making a purely synchronous design impossible for the stated batch size.

Queueable is the lightest-weight async wrapper that satisfies both constraints:

* **one request per new account** – each loop iteration fires a single POST with a 1-element JSON array.
* **batches of up to 200** – the class chains itself, processing 10 accounts per execution, so the limit is never breached.
* **immediate** – `System.enqueueJob` fires the first execution within seconds; from the user's perspective the sync is "right from the trigger."

